data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Where should carts live: Redis or Postgres?\", \"body\": \"The 4 KB session cookie truncates long carts. Either option moves the cart server-side and leaves only a cart ID in the cookie.\\n\\n- **Redis:** fast reads and writes, and key TTLs expire abandoned carts automatically. Carts are ephemeral, so a Redis restart or eviction can lose them unless persistence is configured. Choose it if Redis is already in your stack.\\n- **Postgres:** durable, transactional, and queryable (abandoned-cart reports, joins to orders and inventory). It needs a cleanup job for old carts. Choose it if Postgres is already your system of record and you don't want another service to run.\\n\\nRecommendation: Postgres, unless you already run Redis and want TTL expiry for free. It adds no new infrastructure and keeps carts durable.\", \"actions\": [{\"label\":\"Postgres (recommended)\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"recommended\":true},{\"label\":\"Redis\",\"kind\":\"resolve\"}]}"}}

